.TH WMPOSXY 1 "September 2025" "wmposxy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wmposxy \- honour X11 --geometry positions under Wayland via XWayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmposxy
.I program
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B wmposxy
utility launches an X11 client (typically running under XWayland) and
repositions the resulting top-level window according to the positional
portion of the client's
.IR --geometry 
argument.
.PP
Wayland compositors usually ignore client-supplied coordinates, honouring
only width/height hints.  This wrapper restores the classic X11 behaviour
by watching for the client's window, then issuing an
.B _NET_MOVERESIZE_WINDOW
request (falling back to
.B XMoveWindow
when necessary).  Width and height are left unchanged; only the
X/Y origin is updated.  Negative offsets are interpreted relative to the
right/bottom edges using the window's current size.
.SH OPTIONS
The wrapper understands the following options before the program name:
.TP
.BR -h , \ --help
Print usage information and exit.
.PP
All other arguments are forwarded unchanged to
.IR program .
The program must supply a
.BR --geometry
(or
.BR -geometry )
token in either split form ("--geometry 80x24+0+0") or joined form
("--geometry=80x24+0+0").  If the token is not present, the wrapper
exits with an error and does not launch the client.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X11 display to connect to.  If no display is available the wrapper
terminates with an error.
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
Used only to detect Wayland sessions; the wrapper requires an X11/XWayland
bridge to perform repositioning.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.  On systems without XWayland the wrapper launches the child
process and exits without repositioning.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred (missing geometry token, unable to open the X display,
child exited before creating a window, etc.).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wmctrl (1),
.BR xterm (1),
.BR konsole (1)
.SH SECURITY
Allowing arbitrary window repositioning reintroduces spoofing
possibilities that Wayland deliberately removed.  A malicious client could
place a fake password dialog over a legitimate one.  Use this tool only on
trusted systems.
.SH AUTHORS
Stuart Lynne and ChatGPT, inspired by the
.B wmctrl
utility.
